    "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/ETag",
    "title": "ETag header - HTTP | MDN",
    "snippet": "Jan 9, 2015         The HTTP ETag (entity tag) response header is an identifier for a specific version of a resource. It lets caches be more efficient and save bandwidth, as a web server does not need to resend a full response if the content has not changed. Additionally, ETags help to prevent simultaneous updates of a resource from overwriting each other (\"mid-air collisions\").",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://elementor.com/blog/304/",
    "title": "What is 304 Not Modified Response?",
    "snippet": "Aug 13, 2024         How Browsers Handle Cached Responses. When your browser receives a 304 Not Modified response, it doesn’t simply discard the cached resource. Instead, it performs a series of checks to ensure that the cached copy is still valid and can be used. First, the browser compares the response headers it received with the headers stored in its cache.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_ETag",
    "title": "HTTP ETag - Wikipedia",
    "snippet": "If the ETag values match, meaning that the resource has not changed, the server may send back a very short response with a HTTP 304 Not Modified status. The 304 status tells the client that its cached version is still good and that it should use that.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/58267693/does-the-must-revalidate-cache-control-header-tell-the-browser-to-only-download",
    "title": "apache - Does the must-revalidate cache-control header tell the browser ...",
    "snippet": "In order for this to work, in response to initial request server will send either one or both of: Etag header (which contains hashed value of the content), which client will cache and send back in revalidation request as If-None-Match header, so server can compare clients cached Etag value with the current Etag on server side.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.postaffiliatepro.com/faq/etag-last-modified-headers-caching/",
    "title": "ETag and Last-Modified Headers: Essential HTTP Caching...",
    "snippet": "Jan 13, 2026         Learn how ETag and Last-Modified HTTP headers optimize caching efficiency, reduce bandwidth consumption, and accelerate page rendering in affiliate management systems.If they match, the server responds with a 304 Not Modified status code and an empty body, signaling the browser to use its cached version. If the ETags differ, the server sends the full resource with a 200 OK status code, allowing the browser to update its cache.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.hostney.com/blog/http-304-not-modified-what-it-means-and-why-it-happens",
    "title": "HTTP 304 Not Modified: What It Means and Why It Happens - HOSTNEY",
    "snippet": "Apr 15, 2026         If the file has not changed since that timestamp, the server returns 304. If-None-Match – the browser sends an ETag (a fingerprint of the file’s content) for the version it has cached, and asks the server to compare against the file’s current ETag.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://contabo.com/blog/http-304-status-code/",
    "title": "HTTP 304 Status Code: Meaning and How to Fix It | Contabo Blog",
    "snippet": "Apr 15, 2026         How HTTP Caching Triggers a 304 Response. Two mechanisms control whether a server returns 304 or 200 on repeat requests. The ETag Header.The immediate fix for a single user seeing stale content: clear the browser cache.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.keycdn.com/support/if-modified-since-http-header",
    "title": "If-Modified-Since HTTP Header - KeyCDN Support",
    "snippet": "The If-Modified-Since HTTP header indicates the time for which a browser first downloaded a resource from the server. This helps determine whether or not the resource has changed since the last time it was accessed. If the HTTP status of a particular resource is 304 Not Modified, this means that the file has not changed and there is no need to download it again.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.resellerclub.com/blog/what-is-statuscode-304/",
    "title": "HTTP Status Code 304 - Why It Happens & How It Optimizes...",
    "snippet": "Jun 16, 2025         The 304 status code, also known as “304 Not Modified”, is an HTTP response indicating that the requested resource has not changed since it was last accessed. When a browser requests a webpage or resource that was previously cached, the server can respond with a status HTTP 304 to tell the browser to use its cached version instead of downloading the same content again. This helps reduce bandwidth usage and improves loading times. The HTTP 304 status code is not an error but rather a way to optimise web performance through efficient caching.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.fastly.com/blog/etags-what-they-are-and-how-to-use-them",
    "title": "ETags: What they are, and how to use them | Fastly",
    "snippet": "May 6, 2023         A matching ETag allows the server to return a `304 Not Modified` response. Correct ETag implementation can improve performance and reduce origin traffic. Entity tags, or ETags, are a really useful part of HTTP that can make sites run faster through more efficient caching of resources.",
    "published": null
  }
]
